quirements R3.1 through R3.3 from the same SRD, continuing to build on the existing R1.x and R2.x implementation in the cut command without refactoring the current structure. I'm realizing I need to account for the weight budget constraint mentioned in the instructions. Since I don't have direct access to requirements.yaml, I should assume each requirement has a default weight of 1 unless there's evidence otherwise, and I need to show my arithmetic when proposing tasks.\n\nLooking at the output format requirements, I should structure my response with the summary and reasoning sections first, then the YAML list with proper formatting—making sure strings with colons are quoted and using valid YAML syntax with literal block scalars for descriptions.\n\nLet me finalize the response now, being careful about whether to include the full SRD requirement text or reference it more concisely. I'm realizing the constraint requires a strict 1:1 mapping between SRD requirements and task requirements, so I can't split them into sub-bullets even if I don't have the full SRD content. Since I don't know the actual requirement breakdown, I should just reference the SRD IDs directly with generic descriptions like \"Implement srd026-cut R1.1 as specified in the SRD\" — this keeps things deterministic and ensures consistency across runs.\n\nLooking at the commit message pattern from the orchestrator, this format is exactly what's expected: the task references specific requirement IDs and the stitch agent reads the SRD to understand the implementation details. One more thing to check though — the code_status shows cut and paste as \"not started,\" but the recent commit already created the cut contract with main.go, so I should propose modifying the existing package rather than creating a new one.\n\nFor paste, there's no code yet, so that'll be a creation task. I also need to account for test files — the architecture pattern shows tests go in separate files like cmd/cut/cut_test.go, and t